From feeb4354cc5b54ce4cbaf06a1071b25da92e39a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 04:19:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: no HDR over the tunnel where the check of the
 output found the card changing its pixels

HDR over the tunnel (YCbCr 4:2:2 at 12 bits packed in the RGB 8 bpc pixels of
the Dolby Vision tunnel) is carried exactly only where the graphics card
passes those pixels through unchanged. On an RX 550 (Polaris 12, DCE 11.2,
issue 20 of LibreELEC Hades), HLG over the tunnel came out green, while the
same video in YCbCr 4:2:0 on the ordinary output looked right, and the check
of the Dolby Vision output (hades-output-check, whose CRC measures what the
card does to the bytes) said "not-exact" three times.

So the rule of HDR over the tunnel (HDR10Tunnel.h) has one more "no",
Why::CHECK: where the last report of that check says "not-exact" for this
output, the ordinary output carries the HDR video, and kodi.log says why. The
report holds only while the driver, the output, the running kernel and the
driver's parameters of the tunnel are what they were when it was made
(OutputCheckVerdict.h: the check now writes the kernel and those parameters
into its report); a report made before it said them, or with another kernel
or another value, is not taken, and kodi.log says so once. A result other
than "not-exact" changes nothing.

The help of the setting said that the picture goes out "inside the RGB 8-bit
signal that Dolby Vision uses". The packing is that of Dolby Vision, but the
display is told YCbCr 4:2:2 (the AVI InfoFrame of HDR over the tunnel, and of
Low-Latency, says so): it now says what the display is told, that the video
is exact where the card passes the pixels through unchanged, and that it is
not used where the check found otherwise. Spanish translations of the Estuary
mirror too.

Not tried on hardware.
