--- a/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
+++ b/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
@@ -27618,7 +27618,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadeshdr10tunnel"; translated in Estuary (#33001)
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 msgctxt "#41401"
-msgid "At 3840x2160 and 50 or 60 Hz, HDMI 2.0 does not carry RGB at 10 bits, so the graphics driver sends HDR as YCbCr 4:2:0 at 10 bits (or RGB at 8 bits). With this on, an HDR10, HLG or HDR10+ video goes out as YCbCr 4:2:2 at 12 bits instead, inside the RGB 8-bit signal that Dolby Vision uses, with its HDR metadata as always: the colour of what Hades draws over the video (subtitles, the on-screen display) keeps twice the detail, and the video is carried exactly. It is used only where the display says it takes 4:2:2, the driver can send it and nothing else has the output (Intel and AMD graphics); anything it cannot take goes out as before, and the log says why. It applies with the next video. Off: HDR always goes out as before."
+msgid "At 3840x2160 and 50 or 60 Hz, HDMI 2.0 does not carry RGB at 10 bits, so the graphics driver sends HDR as YCbCr 4:2:0 at 10 bits (or RGB at 8 bits). With this on, an HDR10, HLG or HDR10+ video goes out as YCbCr 4:2:2 at 12 bits instead, with its HDR metadata as always: the display is told that it receives YCbCr 4:2:2, which HDMI carries at the speed of RGB at 8 bits, and Hades packs the picture in it the way it packs Dolby Vision. The colour of what Hades draws over the video (subtitles, the on-screen display) keeps twice the detail, and the video is carried exactly where the graphics card passes the pixels through unchanged. It is used only where the display says it takes 4:2:2, the driver can send it and nothing else has the output (Intel and AMD graphics), and not where \"Check the Dolby Vision output\" found that the card changes the pixels; anything it cannot take goes out as before, and the log says why. It applies with the next video. Off: HDR always goes out as before."
 msgstr ""
 
